cription:\n> \"should add 30% surcharge for a highly enchanted sword (enchantment ≥ 5), increasing base premium to 130 G -- (high enchantment: 100 G + 30 G)\"\n\nThe example shows base premium of 130 G, but then we have additional modifiers. The test expectation was 149 G, but our calculation is 148 G.\n\nLet me check if the first insurance surcharge should be 14 G. 130 * 0.1 = 13.0, which floors/ceilings to 13. Unless there's a business rule we're missing.\n\nPerhaps the order of operations is different. Let me try applying the first insurance surcharge before the loyalty discount but after item modifiers, and ensure we're not double-counting.\n\nWait, I think the issue is in our understanding of the first insurance surcharge. Let me check the example again:\n> \"customer is on a follow-up contract -- each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nThis means the first insurance surcharge applies to every quote, but maybe it's applied to the base premium before other modifiers.\n\nBut in our code, we're applying it after the item-level modifiers (cursed, enchanted) which seems correct.\n\nPerhaps the MHPCO rounds after each modifier. Let me try that:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-866c719310efd3c6","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"  // Apply 20% loyalty discount for customers with 2 or more years\n  if (input.customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD) {\n    finalPremium = finalPremium * (1 - LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_PERCENT);\n  }\n\n  // Apply 10% initial assessment surcharge for first insurance\n  // Note: Each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history\n  // Round up surcharge amount in MHPCO's favor\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = Math.ceil(finalPremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_PERCENT);\n  finalPremium += firstInsuranceSurcharge;","newText":"  // Apply 20% loyalty discount for customers with 2